# Parse Formats Demo

Two small scripts, neither emitting JSON, proving `@code parse=` (class 3
composition work, Part 2): a script's own natural output format binds
directly, no JSON wrapper required just to satisfy `@code`.

## CSV, parsed and rendered as a table

`coverage.sh` prints ordinary CSV. `parse="csv"` binds it as an array of
row objects, exactly like a JSON array would; `@render source=` renders
it the same way regardless of where the array came from.

@code language="bash" src="coverage.sh" label="cov" parse="csv" visible="false" /

@render source="cov" type="table" /

## A plain string, parsed as text, piped straight through

`greeting.sh` prints a sentence, not data. `parse="text"` binds it
verbatim, no parse attempted; `{{ greeting }}` interpolates the string
directly.

@code language="bash" src="greeting.sh" label="greeting" parse="text" visible="false" /

{{ greeting }}

The pipe form (no `label=`, so `parse=` has nothing to bind and is
inert, `parse=` only ever affects labeled binding) is unaffected by any
of this, exactly as it worked before this feature:

@code language="bash" src="greeting.sh" | @render type="code" /
